 returning to that subject and  being involved in neuroscience here at  MIT so I first met Pat and Laurie in  1998 at that time I was chair of the  Department of Biology I had been chair  for about seven years and as anyone  knows leading an organization by the  time you have seven years into an  organization you're covering up your  previous mistakes by making new mistakes  it was time to find another job but the  real motivation that got me interested  in being involved with the McGovern  Institute was that when I took the  chairmanship a biology I wanted to  expand neuroscience MIT I was confident  was under invest  in neuroscience it was a science that  was going to transform our future it was  going to transform how we understood the  human as a biological organism and  mankind as people that interacted if you  think about it it's pretty obvious that  our most unique attribute is our brain  is our ability to think our ability to  learn our ability to act and  understanding the biological basis of  that process and how it evolved and how  we can use it better will be an enormous  transformation in society it'll help us  learn more efficiently communicate more  efficiently it'll be the basis of  economic development as it is now  apparent it'll be the basis of being  able to solve some of the most difficult  medical and mental problems of society  it's transformational and when pat was  introduced to me by Chuck vest and Lori  I was very excited about the fact that  MIT might be able to expand neuroscience  with their excitement and their  resources the other reason it was the  time as a computational power had become  sufficiently powerful that one could  think of the integration of that  technology was molecular and genetic  technology transforming the rate at  which progress was going to be made in  neuroscience so I was invited down to  the IDG headquarters where I met Lori  and Pat on Beacon Street and it was a  exciting moment for me and I think it  was a very exciting moment for them now  the McGovern Institute was building on a  strong palette form at MIT it was too  small but it was strong it was a program  in brain and cognitive  ants that haunts tuber launched in 1964  and then in 1993 the department of brain  and cognitive science was established  here at MIT with Emilio beatsie as its  leader in the center for learning and  memory had been established under the  leadership of Susumu tonegawa so we had  this movement of MIT in establishing  neuroscience here and expanding  neuroscience in the late 90s mid 90s  but with Pat and Laurie's commitment of  350 million dollars forced over 20 years  to establish the McGovern Institute it  became very clear that there was a new  opportunity at MIT to change  neuroscience at this institution so this  is a few slides from 2002 which was our  first board meeting and I found these as  I was thinking about this lecture uh and  this is my first statement about the  McGovern Institute the purpose of the  McGovern Institute is to investigate and  ultimately understand the biological  basis of higher functions of the human  brain now I know this statement was  crafted as a molecular and cell  biologist which is what I am and I made  it clear to the community that I was  excited about leading this but I was not  going to become a neuroscientist because  I didn't think I could do it in the time  that was necessary to actually be a  neuroscientist and make a difference  in that field but I still think that  this is a very powerful objective  biological basis and function of the  human brain but it also is one in which  many other attributes play a role in  leading us to understand in time with  these objectives how the brain works the  excitement of that meeting was the  statement about the launch of the  Institute one of the important decisions  that we made at the beginning working  with Pat and Laurie who had previously  had experience with the Whitehead  Institute is that this I